# Linux Update Utility: maintainer reference

## Architecture

The desktop applications and package-manager backend are implemented in Rust.

- `linux-updater-app/src/lib.rs`: updater/setup windows, five themes, masked
  authorization, legal notice, output display, and UI worker state.
- `linux-updater-app/src/operations.rs`: backend subprocess invocation, script
  execution, streaming stdout/stderr, system information, install and uninstall.
- `scripts/detect-package-managers.sh`: Seth's original handwritten detector.
- `scripts/update-system.sh`: included update command implementation.
- `linux-updater-app/src/backend.rs`: script orchestration, detector-output
  validation, and atomic staging of the included update script.
- `linux-updater-app/src/bin/`: separate updater and setup entry points.
- `linux-updater-app/Cargo.toml` and `Cargo.lock`: pinned GUI dependencies.
- `linux-updater-app/luu-icon.svg`: original icon; `luu-icon.png` is its 128px
  rasterization for the native window icon.

The GUI locates `linux-update-utility` beside its executable and starts it as a
separate process. This works in both the development build and AppDir layout.

Generation sets LINUX_UPDATE_SCRIPT_OUTPUT to a temporary path in the data
directory, then promotes the newly generated script to:

    $XDG_DATA_HOME/linux-update-utility/update-script-output.sh

The default data root is ~/.local/share. Update System generates a fresh script,
checks for the backend's sudo marker, prompts when needed, then runs Bash as the normal user.
The generated script uses a single sudo invocation for system operations;
Flatpak stays unprivileged. Passwords are passed through stdin, never command-line
arguments, environment variables, or generated scripts. Rust-owned password buffers
use zeroize. Cancellation clears the prompt without starting the operation.

The detector runs under trusted Bash with a fixed system PATH. Rust accepts only
known package-manager names whose executable and containing directory share an
owner and are not group- or world-writable. The included update script also fixes
PATH, contains the explicit commands, and uses one sudo boundary. Script staging
is atomic and the generated copy has mode 0600. The backend has no password
parameter and never reads stdin; the GUI writes the password only to sudo's stdin.

Operations run on workers; stdout and stderr are drained concurrently. The UI
stays responsive, prevents overlapping operations, and defers window closing while
a worker is active. Output is bounded in memory. Nonzero exit statuses are errors.
No package-manager operations run during tests.

## Setup and removal

The setup binary loads:

    ../share/linux-update-utility/payload/Linux_Update_Utility-x86_64.AppImage

relative to its executable directory, or the same payload filename beside the
executable for development. It stages the payload, icon and launcher in a private
temporary directory so sudo can read them outside an AppImage's FUSE mount.

Installed paths remain:

    /usr/local/bin/linux-update-utility.AppImage
    /usr/share/applications/linux-update-utility.desktop
    /usr/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/linux-update-utility.svg

The installer copies the AppImage to `.new`, then renames it into place before
installing the launcher and icon. It supports replacing an existing installation
and launches the installed updater as the normal user. Setup reports automatic
launch failures without misreporting installation failure. Temporary staging is
cleaned up when the worker completes.

Settings > Uninstall requires confirmation and authorization, removes only the
three installed paths, and preserves user data. Both windows show the project
copyright and GPLv3 notice.

## Themes

Light, Dark, Green Phosphor, Amber CRT, and Classic Blue use the existing theme IDs.
The config is compatible with the former Qt application's theme setting:

    $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/Seth Langer/Linux Update Utility.conf
    [appearance]
    theme=light

The config root defaults to ~/.config. Unknown themes fall back to Light.

## Build

Use Rust/Cargo 1.88+, CMake 3.16+, pkg-config, and Linux graphics
development libraries. Qt and qmake are no longer dependencies.

Suggested Ubuntu 24.04 build prerequisites (install Rust separately):

    sudo apt install cmake pkg-config libx11-dev libxkbcommon-dev \
      libwayland-dev libgl1-mesa-dev libegl1-mesa-dev patchelf desktop-file-utils \
      squashfs-tools file curl wget libfuse2t64

Build official x86_64 artifacts on the oldest supported distribution with its own
runtime libraries. Keep generic x86-64 flags; never use `-march=native` or Rust
`target-cpu=native` for distributed artifacts. Do not claim cross-distribution
compatibility until the resulting binaries have been tested there.

From the repository root:

    cmake -S linux-updater-app -B linux-updater-app/build
    cmake --build linux-updater-app/build -j2
    ./linux-updater-app/build/linux-updater-app
    ./linux-updater-app/build/luu-setup-utility

CMake runs Cargo with --locked --release and places all three executables in the
build directory. Cargo's intermediates live in
build/cargo. Cargo-only builds do not create the complete runtime layout.

    cargo fmt --manifest-path linux-updater-app/Cargo.toml --check
    cargo clippy --locked --manifest-path linux-updater-app/Cargo.toml --all-targets -- -D warnings
    cargo test --locked --manifest-path linux-updater-app/Cargo.toml
    bash -n build-appimages.sh
    bash -n linux-updater-app/package-appimage.sh
    bash -n linux-updater-app/package-setup-utility.sh

## Packaging

    ./build-appimages.sh

This builds both applications, downloads linuxdeploy if absent, packages the
updater, then embeds that exact updater into the setup AppImage. Outputs:

    input-release/Linux_Update_Utility-x86_64.AppImage
    linux-updater-app/release/LUU_Setup_Utility-x86_64.AppImage

For individual packaging:

    ./linux-updater-app/package-appimage.sh linux-updater-app/build input-release
    ./linux-updater-app/package-setup-utility.sh linux-updater-app/build

The setup packager accepts an explicit payload as argument 2 and release directory
as argument 3. Otherwise input-release must contain exactly one *.AppImage, with
any filename. The embedded copy gets the canonical payload filename. Individual
packagers refuse to overwrite existing outputs. The combined release script stages
artifacts in temporary directories before replacing its known output paths.

The shared package-rust-runtime.sh helper explicitly includes X11, xkbcommon,
and Wayland libraries loaded dynamically by winit. ELF scanning alone misses
these libraries. Both packagers use the same helper.

LINUXDEPLOY can override the packaging tool path. APPIMAGE_EXTRACT_AND_RUN=1 avoids
requiring FUSE while building. On Arch-family builders, the combined script sets
NO_STRIP=1 to accommodate newer RELR libraries. Qt deployment plugins are not used.
Host OpenGL drivers are still required by the egui glow renderer.

## Manual release checks

- Open both windows on X11 and Wayland; check scaling, icons and text input.
- Check all five themes, restart persistence, and the legal notice.
- Generate a script and inspect it before authorizing any real updates.
- Exercise password cancellation, incorrect password, process launch failure,
  update output, nonzero status, and repeated update attempts in a disposable VM.
- Check close attempts during an active operation.
- Exercise setup, replacement, menu launcher, and uninstall in a disposable VM.
- Verify setup payload hash matches the tested updater AppImage.
- Test desktop accessibility and on-screen keyboard behavior. The previous
  Qt-specific request to show a desktop keyboard is no longer used; the Rust
  window/input stack must be validated on target desktops before release.

Do not execute real updates, install, or uninstall as part of automated smoke tests.

## Release maintenance

Footer release text is in src/lib.rs; package version is in Cargo.toml and the
project version in CMakeLists.txt. Keep Cargo.lock committed. Update documentation
when paths or behavior change. Backend changes require tests for command selection,
shell quoting, private file permissions, and privilege separation.
